Inspect identity|id|Prints the current user's UID, primary group and supplementary groups.|Check the credentials involved in a permission failure.|uid is the user identity; gid is the primary group; groups lists additional memberships.|uid=1000(app) gid=1000(app) groups=1000(app),1001(readers)|Read-only; run in the same identity context as the failing process.
Inspect path permissions|namei -l /srv/app/config.yml|Walks a path and displays permissions and ownership for each component.|Locate a parent directory that blocks traversal despite readable file permissions.|Each row is a path component; directory execute permission is required for traversal.|drwx------ root root srv|Read-only; replace the example path. namei is commonly supplied by util-linux.
Disk capacity|df -hT|Reports mounted-filesystem block capacity with readable units and filesystem type.|Identify the full filesystem before cleaning or expanding storage.|Use% is block utilization; Mounted on identifies the mount, not just a directory name.|Filesystem Type Size Used Avail Use% Mounted on|Read-only; compare the mount containing the failing path.
Inode capacity|df -i|Reports inode allocation for mounted filesystems.|Detect no-space failures caused by many small files.|IUse% tracks inode use; free bytes can remain when IFree reaches zero.|Filesystem Inodes IUsed IFree IUse% Mounted on|Read-only; inode semantics vary by filesystem.
Memory headroom|free -h|Summarizes memory and swap accounting.|Distinguish reclaimable cache from genuine memory shortage.|Available is estimated usable headroom; free alone excludes useful reclaimable cache.|Mem: total used free shared buff/cache available|Read-only; correlate with pressure and swap activity.
Process states|ps -eo pid,ppid,user,stat,etime,comm|Prints selected fields for every visible process.|Find parent-child relationships, zombies and blocked tasks.|STAT includes R for runnable, S for interruptible sleep, D for uninterruptible sleep and Z for zombie; ETIME is elapsed lifetime.|PID PPID USER STAT ELAPSED COMMAND|Read-only; visibility depends on namespace and permissions.
Listening sockets|ss -lntp|Lists listening TCP sockets numerically and requests process information.|Check whether a service listens on the expected address and port.|-l selects listeners, -n avoids name lookup, -t selects TCP, -p adds process data when permitted.|LISTEN 0 128 127.0.0.1:8080 0.0.0.0:*|Read-only; loopback binding is not external reachability.
System activity|vmstat 1 5|Samples process, memory, paging, I/O and CPU counters five times.|Separate CPU contention, swapping and I/O waits.|The first row summarizes since boot; later rows cover intervals. r is runnable tasks, b is blocked tasks, si/so are swap traffic.|r b swpd free buff cache si so bi bo in cs us sy id wa st|Read-only; interpret several signals together rather than one column.
Kernel events|journalctl -k --since '-15 min' --no-pager|Shows recent kernel journal messages.|Find OOM, device and filesystem evidence near an incident.|-k selects kernel messages; --since bounds time. Match timestamps and affected devices or cgroups.|kernel: ... Out of memory ...|Read-only; access and retention vary, and logs may contain sensitive metadata.
Mount inspection|findmnt -o TARGET,SOURCE,FSTYPE,OPTIONS|Displays mounted filesystems and selected fields.|Find read-only mounts or unexpected backing devices.|TARGET is the path; SOURCE is the backing object; ro in OPTIONS means read-only.|/data /dev/mapper/vg-data ext4 ro,relatime|Read-only; do not remount until the reason for read-only state is understood.
Open deleted files|lsof +L1|Lists open files with link counts below one.|Explain disk space retained after a log was deleted.|Look at COMMAND, PID, SIZE/OFF and NAME marked deleted; the process still holds the inode.|app 123 app 4w REG ... /var/log/app.log (deleted)|Read-only but may require elevated visibility; do not kill a process solely from this output.
System resolver|getent hosts example.com|Queries the configured name-service lookup path.|Compare application-style resolution with direct DNS tools.|Returned addresses follow system NSS behavior and can include local overrides.|192.0.2.10 example.com|Read-only; example addresses are illustrative and actual results differ.
